  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $12,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${60}^{\u2218}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=12,\\,\\theta ={60}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061({60}^{\u2218}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061({60}^{\u2218}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6\\sqrt{3}).$",
